\section{Macro-Anatomical Sub-Engines}
BNSE-X is composed of three sovereign macro-engines:

\subsection{Cerebrum Simulation Core (CSC)}
The CSC models high-level cognition, decision-making, and associative
processing. Let $\bm{x}(t)$ denote the cortical state vector across lobar
partitions. CSC evolves according to:
\[
\frac{d\bm{x}}{dt} = \bm{F}(\bm{x}, \bm{w}, t),
\]
where $\bm{w}$ denotes synaptic weight tensors.

\subsection{Cerebellum Coordination Engine (CCE)}
The CCE governs motor coordination, timing, and predictive correction. Its
state evolution is defined by:
\[
\bm{y}(t+\Delta t) = \bm{y}(t) + \Delta t \cdot \bm{G}(\bm{y}, \bm{u}),
\]
with $\bm{u}$ representing proprioceptive and vestibular feedback channels.

\subsection{Brainstem Autonomic Core (BAC)}
The BAC maintains autonomic stability and homeostatic regulation. Let
$\bm{z}(t)$ denote autonomic state variables (respiration, heart rate,
thermoregulation). BAC enforces:
\[
\frac{d\bm{z}}{dt} = \bm{H}(\bm{z}) + \bm{\eta}(t),
\]
where $\bm{\eta}(t)$ is a stochastic physiological noise model.

\section{Lobar Processing Matrix}
BNSE-X partitions cortical computation into four lobar modules:

\begin{itemize}
